{:edb/db-before-t 5397 :edb/db-after-t 5398 :edb/tx-data [[13194139538710 50 #inst "2026-10-22T00:09:37.514Z" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186672840 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186672845 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186672857 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186672867 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186672868 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186229181 1040 #edb/ref 17592186557625 13194139538710 17592186045443 false] [17592186229181 1040 #edb/ref 17592186672875 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672874 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672840 1071 "A brewing tank initially holds $360\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672840 1072 #uuid "1b80a0f4-7801-535e-bc20-eae6ddee5773" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672840 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $360\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{30}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30}+10.8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30}+10.8 & =6 \\\\ A & =-4.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10.8-4.8{e}^{-t/30}.\n$$" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672846 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672855 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672861 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672865 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672870 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1077 "selection" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672841 1078 #edb/r