{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =1.5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+21.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+21 & =18 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21-3{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672846 1080 "Q(t)=8.4-4.4e^{-t/14}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672846 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672846 1083 #uuid "44b353c1-c2ee-54c3-b666-ccb5c859c5f6" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672847 1080 "Q(t)=16-e^{-t/80}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672847 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672847 1083 #uuid "4a120c58-a242-55df-ae6b-10b365f315f4" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672848 1080 "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/24}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672848 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672848 1083 #uuid "4a75fa10-6da7-5407-9c8d-f142fc65c9f9" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672847 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672853 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672856 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672862 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672871 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1077 "selection" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1078 #edb/ref 17592186672871 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672849 1079 #uuid "4cab0c71-5a81-5ddc-9dfe-02c242f74b7b" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672850 1080 "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672850 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672850 1083 #uuid "4cf6c63e-69eb-5222-be8b-10c3f1be8e3b" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [1759218667285