te that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.3-\\frac{Q}{80}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80}Q=0.3.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/80},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{80}\\alpha =0.3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/80}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/80}+24 & =15 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-9{e}^{-t/80}.\n$$" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672858 1080 "Q(t)=10+4e^{-t/40}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672858 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672858 1083 #uuid "9071fcc3-5fc2-5104-a059-5fc677809145" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672859 1080 "Q(t)=10+5e^{-t/25}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672859 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672859 1083 #uuid "91d23fe2-88cf-561c-a0e4-1741996358a3" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672860 1080 "Q(t)=10.8-0.8e^{-t/24}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672860 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672860 1083 #uuid "9294db4d-c049-5c7e-9775-3e4ab32bdfb2" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672861 1080 "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/14}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672861 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672861 1083 #uuid "996a9731-acc5-5b69-8d22-f2fd2ac623d4" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672862 1080 "Q(t)=24-9e^{-t/16}" 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672862 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538710 17592186045443 true] [17592186672862 1083 #uu